# verify-routing-register.yaml -- declared tier anchors for every verify-leg routing
# surface (backlog v3.0-77; the router-side backstop for the 2026-07-29 tier-promotion
# incident). check-substrate.py's anti-promotion pin protects the POLICY
# (substrate_gate_ok); a downstream instance's promotion never touched the policy -- it
# edited the ROUTING (driver dispatch, backend passthrough, bridge direction) so a
# routine leg could only ever reach a vendor-pinned verifier. These rows declare, per
# routing surface, the one short semantic source line that EMBODIES its tier declaration;
# deploy/check-verify-routing.py asserts each marker is still present. A missing marker
# is a BLOCKING finding: either the tier declaration was edited (the promotion class --
# revert it) or a legitimate refactor moved it (update the row IN THE SAME commit, citing
# the decision that authorized the change). Raising a leg's tier is a decision, not a fix.
#
# Honest residual (recorded, not hidden): marker presence catches edits TO the declared
# tier lines -- the incident's exact shape -- not every conceivable routing rewrite
# around them. The runtime backstop stays check-substrate.py (policy pin, 47/47 since v3.0-233) and the
# journaled substrate blocks (F17/F18) on every leg.
#
# Row schema: id, file, marker (exact substring, one line), means, decision-ref.
#
# THE DIRECTION RULE (v3.0-233, engine half; recorded here so a rewrite of the routing is
# visible the same way a tier edit is). The verifier for a leg is chosen from the
# ARTIFACT's stamped author -- the dispatch stamp's vendor/model for a compile leg, the
# views' verified: absorb fields (or the operator's --author-vendor attestation) for a
# content audit -- never from the session, by check-substrate.choose_verifier, tiered
# exactly like the gate:
#   routine (compile verify, routing leg): the OPPOSITE vendor when its CLI is present;
#     else the SAME vendor with a DIFFERENT model (VERIFY_MODEL, or "<vendor>_second" in
#     ~/.rheoscope/frontier-models.json); else refuse. Never a same-model leg. (While the
#     shipped bridge refuses a same-vendor requester, the same-vendor route is selected
#     and refused with that reason: BRIDGE_SAME_VENDOR_LEGS.)
#   migration / design (content audits, design gates): the opposite vendor, or refuse.
# Every leg passes --direction <chosen> --requester-vendor <author vendor> to verify-cli.js
# and records the route in the packet and the receipt. The rows marked (v3.0-233) anchor
# that rule; moving the direction choice back to a literal or to the session trips them.

rows:
  - id: driver-routine-dispatch
    file: deploy/compile-driver.py
    marker: 'gate_kind="routine"'
    means: the ordinary wired-compile verify leg dispatches at ROUTINE tier (model_id separation suffices)
    decision-ref: v3.0-65 build spec; v3.0-76 (tier doctrine)
  - id: backends-gate-passthrough
    file: deploy/compile-backends.py
    marker: "self.gate_kind"
    means: the backend passes its CONSTRUCTED gate kind through to substrate_gate_ok -- never a hardcoded stricter literal
    decision-ref: v3.0-65 build spec
  - id: substrate-routine-branch
    file: deploy/check-substrate.py
    marker: "return absorb_model != verify_model"
    means: ROUTINE tier is model_id difference, same-vendor compliant
    decision-ref: v3.0-76 (anti-promotion pin guards this branch's semantics)
  - id: substrate-firewall-branch
    file: deploy/check-substrate.py
    marker: "return absorb_vendor != verify_vendor"
    means: MIGRATION/DESIGN tiers require vendor difference (the cross-substrate firewall stays intact -- de-promotion is watched too)
    decision-ref: v3.0-76
  - id: bridge-direction-doc
    file: .claude/skills/bridge/verify-cli.js
    marker: "anthropic: path.join(__dirname, 'verify-server.js'),"
    means: the bridge keeps BOTH answering directions selectable per call (--direction); a vendor-pin rewrite of the bridge (the surface a downstream instance's edit touched) removes this line. (The pre-v3.0.63 marker "routes to an OpenAI Codex/GPT verifier" no longer exists in the shipped bridge -- an adopted register still carrying it reports BLOCKING until this row is updated.)
    decision-ref: v3.0-77; v3.0-233 (bridge half, v3.0.63)
  - id: substrate-direction-opposite-first
    file: deploy/check-substrate.py
    marker: '_OPPOSITE_ORDER = {"openai": ("anthropic",), "anthropic": ("openai",)}'
    means: (v3.0-233) a leg is routed to the OPPOSITE vendor of the artifact's author first, on every tier
    decision-ref: v3.0-233 (audit 2026-10-06 root cause 1; five-pass run 2026-10-07)
  - id: substrate-vendor-tier-no-fallback
    file: deploy/check-substrate.py
    marker: 'return refuse("this is a vendor-tier leg (%s): the verifier must be a different VENDOR "'
    means: (v3.0-233) a migration/design leg never falls back to the author's vendor -- it refuses
    decision-ref: v3.0-233; v3.0-76
  - id: backends-route-from-stamp
    file: deploy/compile-backends.py
    marker: "self.absorb_vendor, self.absorb_model_id, self.gate_kind, avail,"
    means: (v3.0-233) the compile verify and routing legs choose the verifier from the DISPATCH STAMP's author and the constructed gate kind, never the session
    decision-ref: v3.0-233; v3.0-183
  - id: backends-direction-passed
    file: deploy/compile-backends.py
    marker: '"--direction", route["direction"],'
    means: (v3.0-233) the chosen direction is passed to verify-cli.js explicitly (no ambient default)
    decision-ref: v3.0-233
  - id: audit-route-vendor-tier
    file: deploy/audit-content.py
    marker: "route = _substrate.choose_verifier(vendor, model, _substrate.MIGRATION, available,"
    means: (v3.0-233) every content-audit leg is routed by ITS author at the vendor tier (audit-content-v2 reuses this function)
    decision-ref: v3.0-233
